Let's analyze the coordinates of the corners.
The corners of the square identified by $(i, j)$ are:
$(i+0.5, j+0.5)$
$(i+0.5, j-0.5)$
$(i-0.5, j+0.5)$
$(i-0.5, j-0.5)$
This implies the square is the region $[i-0.5, i+0.5] \times [j-0.5, j+0.5]$.
The center of this square is $(i, j)$.
The condition is that *all four points* are within distance $R$ from the origin.
The distance of a point $(x, y)$ from the origin is $\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$.
So we need $\sqrt{(i \pm 0.5)^2 + (j \pm 0.5)^2} \le R$.
Since the maximum distance from the origin to any point in the square $[i-0.5, i+0.5] \times [j-0.5, j+0.5]$ occurs at one of the corners, the condition is equivalent to saying that the farthest corner from the origin is within distance $R$.
The farthest corner from the origin $(0,0)$ among the four corners of the square $(i, j)$ is the one with the largest absolute values of coordinates.
Specifically, the distance squared is $(i \pm 0.5)^2 + (j \pm 0.5)^2$.
To satisfy the condition for *all* four corners, we need the maximum of these distances to be $\le R$.
The maximum distance squared is $(|i| + 0.5)^2 + (|j| + 0.5)^2$.
So the condition is $(|i| + 0.5)^2 + (|j| + 0.5)^2 \le R^2$.

